(a) Generally. No license for the wholesale or retail sale of distilled spirits shall be issued where an entrance to the place of business is located as follows:
(1) Within 100 yards of the property line of a private residence, unless such residence is in a C-2 zoning district;
(2) Within 100 yards of the property line of any public library;
(3) Within 100 yards of the property line of any church, chapel of a mortuary, or other place used primarily for religious services;
(4) Within 200 yards of the property line of any school ground or college campus;
(5) Within 100 yards of the entrance to any MARTA rapid transit station; or
(6) Within 100 yards of the entrance to an existing establishment that is licensed to sell alcoholic beverages at wholesale or at retail for consumption off the premises;
(7) Within 100 yards of the entrance to an alcoholic treatment center owned and operated by the state or any county or municipal government. As authorized by O.C.G.A. § 3-3-21, the provisions of this subsection shall not apply at any location within 100 yards of an alcoholic treatment center owned and operated by the state or any county or municipal government which was licensed prior to July 1, 1981.
(b) Issuance or denial of license. The city commissioners may, in their discretion, issue or deny any license for the sale of distilled spirits where there is evidence that the type and number of schools in the vicinity causes minors to frequent the immediate area, even though there is compliance with minimum distance from schools.
(Code 1967, § 4-57; Ord. No. O-05-16, Art. III, §§ 1, 2, 12-19-05; Ord. No. O-11-10, 11-21-11)
State Law reference— Sale of alcoholic beverages near churches, schools or college campus, O.C.G.A. § 3-3-21.

